# 0007. Where Codex runs in the flow

- Status: Accepted
- Date: 2026-11-22

Decided by the owner on 2026-11-22: fixed points where Claude always calls Codex
([0006](0006-codex-read-only-second-agent.adr)). No step is left to Claude's judgement:
at each one Codex runs every time, or Claude records why it was skipped.

## Technical details
- **Problem**: a reviewer called "when it seems useful" gets forgotten, so most work goes
  unchecked. Each trigger below is a rule, not a choice.
- **Skips**: only when Codex is unavailable (not installed, not logged in, the call fails).
  Claude then writes `Codex: skipped (<reason>)` where the result would have gone.
- **`github-flow`, every PR**: before pushing the branch, Claude runs the Codex review with
  the read-only command from the `codex` skill
  (`codex review --base origin/main -c sandbox_mode="read-only"` after `git fetch origin main`:
  `review` has no `-s`), verifies
  each finding, commits the fixes, so CI checks the reviewed code, and adds to the PR body:
  ```
  ## Codex review
  - fixed: <finding> (<sha>)
  - dismissed: <finding>: <why>
  ```
  No findings: "Codex review: nothing found". Codex unavailable: "Codex review: skipped
  (<reason>)".
- **`security-audit`, every audit** (audits still start only when I ask): Codex audits the
  same repo read-only and independently. Claude verifies each of its findings and merges the
  confirmed ones into the one Security Report, marked `found by: Codex`; unconfirmed ones
  are listed as dismissed with the reason.
- **`adr-manager`, every ADR draft**: Codex critiques the draft before I see it (weak
  trade-offs, missing alternatives, gaps in the details). Claude adds the points it accepts
  and lists the rest, with reasons, in its message to me. Codex never approves anything.
- **Every bug fix** (`bug/` and `hotfix/` branches): before fixing, Claude asks Codex for its
  own root-cause analysis of the bug, compares it with its own, and says in the PR body
  where they agreed or differed. Codex doesn't fix anything.
- **Files**: one line in each of `github-flow`, `security-audit` and `adr-manager` pointing
  to the `codex` skill; workflow and engineering version bumps; CHANGELOG line.
- **Consequences**: every PR, ADR, audit and bug fix has a second-model check on record.
  Each one adds a few minutes and some quota; a `minor/` one-line PR still gets its review.

## Technologies and alternatives
- Alternatives:
  - Codex in CI on every push: spends quota on every push and needs an OpenAI key in repo
    secrets.
  - Codex when Claude judges it useful: rejected by me; it must run each time.
  - Codex only when I ask: easy to forget, so most PRs go unreviewed.
